          title: "The child in the midst — status, not sweetness",
          body: "The disciples ask the org-chart question — *who is greatest?* — and Jesus answers with a person: a **παιδίον**, a young child, stood in the middle of the seminar. To hear the answer rightly, strip away nineteen centuries of sentiment about childhood innocence, because the ancient world had none of it: a child in that culture was not a symbol of purity or wonder but of **zero status** — no rights, no achievements, no leverage, no vote, wholly dependent on someone else's goodwill, and (crucially for the parallel) *unembarrassed about the dependence*. That is the quality named in v. 4: ταπεινώσει ἑαυτόν, whoever will *lower himself* like this child. The command is not ”be naive” or ”be trusting by temperament” — it is a demand aimed precisely at the question asked: stop computing rank. You do not enter this kingdom carrying your seniority; the entrance is sized for people who arrive with nothing to declare, the way a child arrives everywhere. Notice too the reversal of religious machinery in v. 5: greatness is not even *being* the child — it is **receiving** one, in his name; the kingdom's promotion path runs through welcoming precisely the people who can add nothing to your standing. The disciples asked who would be greatest. Jesus' full answer, unrolled across this whole chapter, is: the question itself is what you must be converted from.",
