          lemma: "μυρίων ταλάντων … ἑκατὸν δηνάρια … ἀπὸ τῶν καρδιῶν",
          title: "Ten thousand talents — the arithmetic of the two debts",
          body: "The numbers are the sermon; do them. A **denarius** is a day's wage. A **talent** is six thousand denarii — roughly twenty years of labour. **Ten thousand talents** is therefore some *sixty million working days*: two hundred thousand years of wages, more than the annual tribute of all Roman Syria, a figure chosen to be laughable — μύριοι is also Greek's word for ”countless,” the largest number the language names in one word. No slave could owe it; no slave could repay it; the man's ”be patient and I will repay everything” is the comedy of total insolvency still doing instalment maths. Against this: **a hundred denarii** — real money, three months' pay, the kind of debt that genuinely stings, which is what makes the parable honest; the wrongs we are asked to forgive are not nothing. But the ratio is six hundred thousand to one, and the forgiven man walks *straight out* (ἐξελθών — same hour, mercy still wet on him) and takes his colleague **by the throat**. The audience of fellow slaves is ”terribly grieved” — the parable's model for how forgiven communities should feel watching a forgiven person refuse to forgive. The ending is the New Testament at its most severe: the pardon is *revoked*, the torturers come, and Jesus signs it personally — *so also my heavenly Father will do to you*. This is the fifth petition of his own prayer (6:12) told as horror story: the unforgiving heart is not denied grace so much as rendered incapable of holding it — a vessel that will not pour cannot, it turns out, stay full. And the last phrase closes every loophole of technique: **ἀπὸ τῶν καρδιῶν ὑμῶν** — from your hearts. Not the words of forgiveness; the thing itself.",
